      "raw_text": "1/\nAlex Magno calls it stagnation.\nBut it’s worse than that — it’s a slow-motion collapse masked by indifference.\nWhen the economy, governance, and public spirit all fail together, history doesn’t repeat — it unravels. 🇵🇭\n🧵 #Philippines  #Philstar #Stagnant\n\n2/\nSo this is how a nation dies.\nThe Philippines now holds two unwanted titles: worst-performing stock market and worst-performing currency in ASEAN. Both mirror one thing — the collapse of confidence.\n\n3/\nGDP growth crashed from 5.5% to 4%.\nForeign direct investments fell >40%.\nTourism lags behind neighbors.\nAgriculture remains stagnant — obsessed with price controls instead of productivity. The result: rising imports, weaker peso, and vanishing food security.\n\n4/\nOur exports face 19% US tariffs while Thailand and Malaysia enjoy tariff-free access.\nThe “strong negotiator” failed to secure trade advantages — another blow to an already struggling economy.\n\n5/\nDebt keeps climbing as growth slows.\nGovernment borrows to fund food subsidies.\nIf this continues, a credit-rating downgrade is inevitable — pushing borrowing costs higher and the peso lower.\n\n6/\nEducation is collapsing.\nFunctional literacy has dropped sharply.\nMalnutrition is eroding the next generation’s capacity to compete. The country isn’t just poor — it’s getting less capable of escaping poverty.\n\n7/\nConfidence is gone.\nEven with lower interest rates, bank lending shrinks.\nBusinesses are shelving expansion plans.\nInvestors, consumers, and workers all see one direction — downward.\n\n8/\nAs in past crises, Filipinos will leave again.\nA new wave of migration looms — the skilled, the young, the hopeful.\nWhen a state can’t feed or inspire its people, its best citizens quietly pack their bags.\n\n9/\nPublic outrage? None.\nPeople are too exhausted to protest.\nCorruption scandals pile up while the President offers discounts on train fares as if populism could replace policy.\n\n10/\nApproval ratings: 18% — the lowest for any sitting president in decades.\nPolitical capital: zero.\nNo reforms, no direction, no urgency.\nJust drift — and a nation sinking with it.\nhttps://t.co/NQIt5x6q07",
      "cleaned_text": "1/ Alex Magno calls it stagnation. But it’s worse than that — it’s a slow-motion collapse masked by indifference. When the economy, governance, and public spirit all fail together, history doesn’t repeat — it unravels. 🇵🇭 🧵 #Philippines #Philstar #Stagnant 2/ So this is how a nation dies. The Philippines now holds two unwanted titles: worst-performing stock market and worst-performing currency in ASEAN. Both mirror one thing — the collapse of confidence. 3/ GDP growth crashed from 5.5% to 4%. Foreign direct investments fell >40%. Tourism lags behind neighbors. Agriculture remains stagnant — obsessed with price controls instead of productivity. The result: rising imports, weaker peso, and vanishing food security. 4/ Our exports face 19% US tariffs while Thailand and Malaysia enjoy tariff-free access. The “strong negotiator” failed to secure trade advantages — another blow to an already struggling economy. 5/ Debt keeps climbing as growth slows. Government borrows to fund food subsidies. If this continues, a credit-rating downgrade is inevitable — pushing borrowing costs higher and the peso lower. 6/ Education is collapsing. Functional literacy has dropped sharply. Malnutrition is eroding the next generation’s capacity to compete. The country isn’t just poor — it’s getting less capable of escaping poverty. 7/ Confidence is gone. Even with lower interest rates, bank lending shrinks. Businesses are shelving expansion plans. Investors, consumers, and workers all see one direction — downward. 8/ As in past crises, Filipinos will leave again. A new wave of migration looms — the skilled, the young, the hopeful. When a state can’t feed or inspire its people, its best citizens quietly pack their bags. 9/ Public outrage? None. People are too exhausted to protest. Corruption scandals pile up while the President offers discounts on train fares as if populism could replace policy. 10/ Approval ratings: 18% — the lowest for any sitting president in decades. Political capital: zero. No reforms, no direction, no urgency. Just drift — and a nation sinking with it.",
      "english_translation": "1/ Alex Magno calls it stagnation. But it’s worse than that — it’s a slow-motion collapse masked by indifference. When the economy, governance, and public spirit all fail together, history doesn’t repeat — it unravels. 🇵🇭 🧵 #Philippines #Philstar #Stagnant 2/ So this is how a nation dies. The Philippines now holds two unwanted titles: worst-performing stock market and worst-performing currency in ASEAN. Both mirror one thing — the collapse of confidence. 3/ GDP growth crashed from 5.5% to 4%. Foreign direct investments fell >40%. Tourism lags behind neighbors. Agriculture remains stagnant — obsessed with price controls instead of productivity. The result: rising imports, weaker peso, and vanishing food security. 4/ Our exports face 19% US tariffs while Thailand and Malaysia enjoy tariff-free access. The “strong negotiator” failed to secure trade advantages — another blow to an already struggling economy. 5/ Debt keeps climbing as growth slows. Government borrows to fund food subsidies. If this continues, a credit-rating downgrade is inevitable — pushing borrowing costs higher and the peso lower. 6/ Education is collapsing. Functional literacy has dropped sharply. Malnutrition is eroding the next generation’s capacity to compete. The country isn’t just poor — it’s getting less capable of escaping poverty. 7/ Confidence is gone. Even with lower interest rates, bank lending shrinks. Businesses are shelving expansion plans. Investors, consumers, and workers all see one direction — downward. 8/ As in past crises, Filipinos will leave again. A new wave of migration looms — the skilled, the young, the hopeful. When a state can’t feed or inspire its people, its best citizens quietly pack their bags. 9/ Public outrage? None. People are too exhausted to protest. Corruption scandals pile up while the President offers discounts on train fares as if populism could replace policy. 10/ Approval ratings: 18% — the lowest for any sitting president in decades. Political capital: zero. No reforms, no direction, no urgency. Just drift — and a nation sinking with it.",
